WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:09.160
In December 2018, for the first time,
teacher strikes ensnared a charter

00:00:09.170 --> 00:00:13.730
school network. This was seen by many as
another sign that unions are coming for

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:16.880
charter schools.
Indeed, unions have worked avidly to

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:20.900
organize more charter school teachers.
Are they succeeding? Nope!

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:25.100
During the past decade, the share of
unionized charters has actually declined.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:29.960
What's the story? Well, it's tough for
unions to organize charter teachers when

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:34.190
union leaders are busy treating charter
schools as a political pinata, and a

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:38.390
target during teacher strikes and
legislative fights. It's also not clear

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:41.420
that unions think charters are ultimately
worth the trouble!

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:45.769
Charter schools tend to be small, account
for just a tiny fraction of all teachers,

00:00:45.769 --> 00:00:49.879
and typically require unions to work
school-by-school, rather than by

00:00:49.879 --> 00:00:53.479
organizing whole school districts at a
time. Charters have much to offer

00:00:53.479 --> 00:00:57.649
teachers frustrated by district
bureaucracies, but this has been too

00:00:57.649 --> 00:01:03.170
rarely celebrated  — or communicated — by
advocates. Whatever the unions do, that's

00:01:03.170 --> 00:01:06.240
one place charters need to do much
better.

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:08.720
Do you think it's good or bad for

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:13.300
charter schools to be unionized? Let us
know in our poll. Also, let us know what

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:17.380
other topics you'd like our scholars to
cover in 60 seconds, and be sure to like

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:22.900
and subscribe for more research and
videos from AEI.

